WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.537 --> 00:00:03.478
- I was very self-motivated
when I was in school.

00:00:03.478 --> 00:00:05.326
I was taking acting classes at night,

00:00:05.326 --> 00:00:07.253
I was going to Summer Stock Theater.

00:00:07.253 --> 00:00:10.880
- You take the blue pill, the story ends,

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:11.898
you wake up in your bed

00:00:11.898 --> 00:00:14.017
and believe whatever you want to believe.

00:00:14.017 --> 00:00:18.515
- Since I was a child I
was always asking why.

00:00:18.515 --> 00:00:19.608
They want you to just
to dive off your board

00:00:19.608 --> 00:00:20.469
into the ground.

00:00:20.469 --> 00:00:24.426
I got bumps and cuts,
and boards, and elbows.

00:00:24.426 --> 00:00:27.771
Your life experience is a resource.

00:00:27.771 --> 00:00:30.201
It's kind of a resource,
it's what you can bring.

00:00:30.201 --> 00:00:32.757
Happiness is; I guess I could only do

00:00:32.757 --> 00:00:36.139
like kind of an emotive run on
that which is, it's all good.

00:00:36.139 --> 00:00:40.151
- He's a Canadian actor,
producer, director and musician.

00:00:40.151 --> 00:00:41.974
He's best known for his acting career,

00:00:41.974 --> 00:00:43.803
which began in 1985,

00:00:43.803 --> 00:00:46.086
and has spanned more than three decades.

00:00:46.086 --> 00:00:48.487
His acting career has
garnered several awards,

00:00:48.487 --> 00:00:51.471
including a star on the
Hollywood Walk of Fame.

00:00:51.471 --> 00:00:53.457
He's Keanu Reeves, and here's my take

00:00:53.457 --> 00:00:55.687
on his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:55.687 --> 00:00:58.919
(jet engine)

00:00:58.919 --> 00:01:02.013
- I left home when I was 17, 18.

00:01:02.013 --> 00:01:06.180
I drove from Toronto Canada
to Los Angeles when I was 20.

00:01:08.257 --> 00:01:11.507
And moved out of my home when I was 18.

00:01:14.685 --> 00:01:16.901
So the beginning,

00:01:16.901 --> 00:01:20.401
I could take care of myself as a teenager.

00:01:21.598 --> 00:01:24.775
When I moved to the
States I had some family,

00:01:24.775 --> 00:01:28.942
and I had a lot of rejection. (laughing)

00:01:30.971 --> 00:01:33.596
- [Interviewer] Who did you want to be?

00:01:33.596 --> 00:01:35.947
How did you foresee
your future as an actor?

00:01:35.947 --> 00:01:38.916
- I didn't, I just wanted to do it.

00:01:38.916 --> 00:01:40.512
I was very self-motivated.

00:01:40.512 --> 00:01:43.869
When I was in school I was
taking acting classes at night,

00:01:43.869 --> 00:01:45.754
I was going to Summer Stock Theater,

00:01:45.754 --> 00:01:48.254
I was doing community theater.

00:01:50.813 --> 00:01:52.136
- [Interviewer] You
were simply enjoying it.

00:01:52.136 --> 00:01:54.665
- I was enjoying it, but it was a kind of;

00:01:54.665 --> 00:01:58.208
I don't want to use the word calling.

00:01:58.208 --> 00:01:59.389
- [Interviewer] Like destiny.

00:01:59.389 --> 00:02:00.364
- I don't know if it was destiny,

00:02:00.364 --> 00:02:02.864
it's just what I wanted to do.

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:05.983
- [Interviewer] Still enjoying it.

00:02:05.983 --> 00:02:07.038
- Yeah, still enjoying it

00:02:07.038 --> 00:02:10.288
and still working at working I call it.

00:02:11.887 --> 00:02:15.100
- He's letting go of his best self

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:17.988
for one, his own kind of
dark passion to fight,

00:02:17.988 --> 00:02:19.653
to want to have power.

00:02:19.653 --> 00:02:21.415
But he's using it as like,

00:02:21.415 --> 00:02:22.807
but I have to take care of the temple.

00:02:22.807 --> 00:02:24.997
I have to take care of my parents,

00:02:24.997 --> 00:02:26.906
I have to take care of my master.

00:02:26.906 --> 00:02:29.884
And so, it's called Man of Tai Chi.

00:02:29.884 --> 00:02:33.146
There's these things that we often times,

00:02:33.146 --> 00:02:34.711
we have responsibilities.

00:02:34.711 --> 00:02:37.498
And so we'll make sacrifices, you know.

00:02:37.498 --> 00:02:39.549
Or we'll be attracted to something,

00:02:39.549 --> 00:02:42.393
and we'll make sacrifices
to get something.

00:02:42.393 --> 00:02:44.539
And the film is kind of promoting the idea

00:02:44.539 --> 00:02:46.832
that we have to be thoughtful about that.

00:02:46.832 --> 00:02:50.332
That you can sometimes sacrifice yourself,

00:02:51.453 --> 00:02:53.037
that you think it's for the good,

00:02:53.037 --> 00:02:56.111
but maybe there's another way.

00:02:56.111 --> 00:02:57.060
Blue pill or red pill?

00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:58.310
Easy, red pill.

00:03:00.886 --> 00:03:01.793
What?

00:03:01.793 --> 00:03:03.558
- You take the blue pill,

00:03:03.558 --> 00:03:06.147
the story ends, you wake up in your bed

00:03:06.147 --> 00:03:08.740
and believe whatever you want to believe.

00:03:08.740 --> 00:03:12.853
You take the red pill,
you stay in wonderland.

00:03:12.853 --> 00:03:16.603
And I show you how deep
the rabbit hole goes.

00:03:18.446 --> 00:03:22.613
Remember, all I'm offering
is the truth, nothing more.

00:03:23.649 --> 00:03:27.816
- The red pill to me is a
kind of symbolic taking up

00:03:30.361 --> 00:03:33.378
of the torch of trying to understand,

00:03:33.378 --> 00:03:34.795
and to, in a way,

00:03:37.822 --> 00:03:39.989
there's an idealism to it.

00:03:40.829 --> 00:03:41.910
It's almost like, to me,

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:45.847
the red pill represents the
best of who we can possibly be.

00:03:45.847 --> 00:03:48.652
To ourselves and to other people.

00:03:48.652 --> 00:03:53.190
To all sentient beings,
to nature, everything.

00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:56.228
I trained for the film
for about two months,

00:03:56.228 --> 00:03:59.364
hanging out with lawyers, going to court,

00:03:59.364 --> 00:04:00.890
working on an accent.

00:04:00.890 --> 00:04:05.158
I did some movement
exercises, just for the guy.

00:04:05.158 --> 00:04:07.485
And so by the time I got to the film,

00:04:07.485 --> 00:04:09.393
I was just ready.

00:04:09.393 --> 00:04:13.060
Since I was a child I
was always asking why.

00:04:14.242 --> 00:04:16.901
It used to drive everyone around me crazy

00:04:16.901 --> 00:04:19.984
because it was like why and how come.

00:04:19.984 --> 00:04:24.350
So once I started working
and making movies,

00:04:24.350 --> 00:04:29.119
and acting in that format, I
was always really curious about

00:04:29.119 --> 00:04:32.634
where the camera was going,
what was the lens doing.

00:04:32.634 --> 00:04:35.549
Also in terms of what
everybody else was doing.

00:04:35.549 --> 00:04:37.042
Oh my God, I know, that's like the thing.

00:04:37.042 --> 00:04:40.347
It's like okay Keanu, the
waves are three feet high,

00:04:40.347 --> 00:04:42.554
you're in Hawaii ocean;
it's just the ocean.

00:04:42.554 --> 00:04:45.572
And we want you to fall off.

00:04:45.572 --> 00:04:48.057
We want you to just dive off
your board into the ground.

00:04:48.057 --> 00:04:51.807
I got bumps and cuts,
and boards, and elbows,

00:04:53.109 --> 00:04:56.364
and jumping and scraping, but that's fun.

00:04:56.364 --> 00:04:59.791
Your life experience is a resource.

00:04:59.791 --> 00:05:03.303
It's kind of a resource;
it's what you can bring.

00:05:03.303 --> 00:05:04.760
And so I kind of describe it as like,

00:05:04.760 --> 00:05:06.990
there's myself and then there's the role,

00:05:06.990 --> 00:05:09.018
and the story that you're telling.

00:05:09.018 --> 00:05:11.821
And you bring what you
bring, and then you learn,

00:05:11.821 --> 00:05:14.678
and you go to places that the
character will take you to.

00:05:14.678 --> 00:05:17.261
I know I'm a pretty simple guy.

00:05:18.936 --> 00:05:22.629
Ever since I was a young
kid I wanted to act.

00:05:22.629 --> 00:05:26.660
I started professional
acting when I was 15.

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:29.627
And I've been really
lucky to have the chance

00:05:29.627 --> 00:05:31.044
to have a career.

00:05:33.872 --> 00:05:36.634
I just have always
tried to keep it simple.

00:05:36.634 --> 00:05:39.514
Happiness is, I guess I can only do

00:05:39.514 --> 00:05:42.763
kind of an emotive run on
that, which is it's all good.

00:05:42.763 --> 00:05:44.960
Everything's good.

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:45.795
You're feeling good,

00:05:45.795 --> 00:05:48.448
you're doing something
that makes you feel good.

00:05:48.448 --> 00:05:52.365
You're working with
people, loving with people.

00:05:56.547 --> 00:05:57.714
It's all good.

